      <StdOut>[xUnit.net 00:00:00.00] xUnit.net VSTest Adapter v4.0.1+0f7d528729 (64-bit .NET 10.0.15)
[xUnit.net 00:00:00.21]   Discovering: Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests
[xUnit.net 00:00:01.29]   Discovered:  Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests
[xUnit.net 00:00:02.03]   Starting:    Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests
[xUnit.net 00:00:08.40]       A visual-viewport scroll listener is called with itself as this. DomBridge.DispatchVisualViewportScrollEvent, in src/Broiler.HtmlBridge.Dom/DomBridge/LayoutMetrics.Scrolling.cs, passes each listener as its own receiver, and has since the dispatch was first written, where the inner invoke of DOM §2.9 calls the callback with the event's currentTarget, which is window.visualViewport.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.VisualViewportScrollDispatchTests.AScrollListenerIsCalledWithTheVisualViewportAsThis' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:15.76]       window.frames is WindowFrames (an exotic object forwarding to window and child frames) rather than window itself, so frames['named'] finds child frames even when shadowed by global vars. See FrameNameTests.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.FrameStructureTests.TheFrameListIsTheWindowItself' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:19.98]       DomBridge/Registration/Window.cs installs the window's own copies of the three EventTarget methods unguarded, declaring 3, 3 and 1 arguments — where the document's (Registration/Document.cs) and every element's (JsObjects.cs) are guarded by _eventTargetRoutingReady and so come from the routed EventTarget.prototype methods, minted with Web IDL's 2, 2, 1 at DomBridge/Events.cs. So window.addEventListener.length is 3 where a browser says 2, and it is a different function object from EventTarget.prototype.addEventListener — which the defensive `EventTarget.prototype.addEventListener.call(window, ...)` idiom needs it to be.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.DomEnumerationAndArityTests.TheWindowsEventTargetMethodsAreTheOnesOnEventTargetPrototype' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:20.32]       getRootNode is minted with a declared length of 1 in DomBridge/JsObjects.cs and DomBridge/JsObjects.NonElementNodes.cs, and on the character-data prototype at DomBridge/NodeInterfaces.cs. DOM §4.4 declares `Node getRootNode(optional GetRootNodeOptions options = {})`, so its only argument is optional and a browser reports 0 — a page feature-detecting composed-tree support by reading el.getRootNode.length gets the wrong answer.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.DomEnumerationAndArityTests.GetRootNodeDeclaresNoRequiredArgument' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:21.26]       The getter returns the assigned string (CanvasRenderingContext2D.Font); HTML and Chromium return the serialized font, without the line-height, and no consumed Broiler.CSS API serializes a font shorthand.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.CanvasTextTests.TheFontSerializesWithoutLineHeight' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:22.96]       CssStyleEngine.ExpandShorthands (Broiler.CSS.Dom 0.1.0-preview.1) passes over tokens before the size it does not recognise and takes everything after the size as the family unchecked, so CanvasFont.TryResolve accepts these; Chromium ignores each one.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.CanvasTextTests.JunkAroundTheSizeIsIgnored' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:23.63]       Relative sizes resolve against the 10px default in CanvasFont.TryResolve because ICanvasHost exposes no computed style for the canvas element; HTML and Chromium resolve them against a connected canvas's computed font-size (16px here).
[xUnit.net 00:00:23.64]       CanvasFont.TryResolve draws in the first family of the list only; Broiler.Graphics' BFontStyle takes one family, and no consumed API falls back along a list. Chromium falls back to serif here.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.CanvasTextTests.RelativeSizesOnAConnectedCanvasFollowItsComputedFont' was skipped in the test run.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.CanvasTextTests.AnUnavailableFamilyFallsBackAlongTheList' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:24.29]       Absolute-size keywords resolve to the 10px default in CanvasFont.TryResolve: no consumed Broiler.CSS or Broiler.Layout public API maps them to pixels. Chromium uses 18px for 'large'.
[xUnit.net 00:00:24.29]       CssStyleEngine.ExpandShorthands (Broiler.CSS.Dom 0.1.0-preview.1) classifies the size with CssLengthParser.ParseToPixels and no viewport, so a vw/vh size, or a calc() holding a percentage, yields no font-size and CanvasFont.TryResolve rejects the assignment; Chromium accepts both. The setter stored '5vw serif' before and drew it at 10px sans-serif.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.CanvasTextTests.AKeywordSizeFollowsTheCssTable' was skipped in the test run.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.CanvasTextTests.AViewportRelativeOrPercentageCalcSizeIsAccepted' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:28.91]       An adopted sheet never reaches the live cascade: the style scope is built only from the &lt;style&gt;/&lt;link&gt; elements found in the tree (DomBridge/ComputedStyle.cs via DomBridge/Css.cs:202-219), and the adopted sheets are emitted as synthetic &lt;style&gt; elements in the serialization pass instead (DomBridge/ComputedStyle.cs), which getComputedStyle never runs.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.AdoptedStyleSheetTests.AnAdoptedSheetsRuleAppliesForExactlyAsLongAsItIsAdopted' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:29.01]       A hole is dropped instead of rejected: the assignment copies the array with the engine's own hole-skipping reader (DomBridge/ComputedStyle.cs, GetArrayElements(withHoles: false)), so [a, , b] silently becomes a two-member list where WebIDL converts the value to sequence&lt;CSSStyleSheet&gt; and throws on the undefined the hole yields.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.AdoptedStyleSheetTests.AdoptingAnArrayWithAHoleIsATypeErrorAndChangesNothing' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:38.00]       A keyword is accepted in any case: CanvasRenderingContext2D.TryParseCompositeOperation lowercases the value before comparing, as the string state did, where HTML requires the keyword to be identical.
[xUnit.net 00:00:38.00]       The argument is converted twice: CanvasBinding.SetGlobalCompositeOperation calls ToJsString once for IsSupportedCompositeOperation and again for the operator it stores, where Web IDL converts a DOMString argument once, so a toString that answers differently the second time decides what is stored.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.CanvasCompositeOperationTests.AKeywordInTheWrongCaseIsIgnored' was skipped in the test run.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.CanvasCompositeOperationTests.TheArgumentIsConvertedToAStringOnce' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:56.78]       A frame's scripts are evaluated in the containing page's global object, and under this engine that object IS the page's window, so a frame's top-level var/function becomes a property of the page's own window as well as of the frame's: frames[0].frameGlobal and the page's unqualified frameGlobal are one binding, and two frames declaring the same name overwrite each other. HTML gives every nested browsing context a global object of its own. src/Broiler.HtmlBridge.Dom/DomBridge/SubDocuments.cs:418
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.FrameScriptExecutionTests.AFramesDeclarationsDoNotLandOnTheContainingPagesWindow' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:00:57.92]       The window-context switch writes the frame's window/document/location/parent onto the realm's global object, and that object IS the containing page's window — which is what `parent` names from inside the frame. So for as long as a frame's script runs, parent.document is the frame's own document and parent.window its own window: a same-origin frame reading or writing its embedder's DOM silently operates on itself, and finds nothing rather than throwing. src/Broiler.HtmlBridge.Dom/Runtime/WindowContextManager.cs:198
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.FrameScriptExecutionTests.AFramesScriptReachesTheContainingPagesDomThroughParent' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:01:04.50]       A frame's Location derives every component from its own URL, so an about:srcdoc frame gets the nonsense serialization "about://" from Scripting/Origin.cs over a URI with no authority (Features/LocationBinding.cs). HTML gives a srcdoc document the origin of the document that embeds it, so location.origin must answer the embedder's — which is the whole of what a framed script tests before it postMessages back, and "about://" matches nothing and equals no other frame's.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.FrameLocationTests.ASrcdocFramesOriginIsTheOneItInheritsFromItsEmbedder' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:01:41.39]       Known defect: an innerHTML rewrite drops the forward registry entry for every node it removes (DomBridge/Traversal.cs: SetElementInnerHtml calls RemoveElementsRecursive, which calls JsObjectRegistry.Remove), so re-inserting a node the page still holds mints a SECOND wrapper: back/byId answer false while isSameNode answers true — one node, two wrappers, both named by the reverse table. removeChild leaves the entry alone, which is why the test above passes and this one does not.
[xUnit.net 00:01:41.65]       A digit-only key misses the store, and it is a third gap in IJsExotic rather than a defect in this module. Both engines route an integer-index key to the indexed hooks, and the contract has a named write hook and no indexed one — so `localStorage[8] = 'x'` becomes an ordinary property and setItem('7', ...) is unreachable as one. Storage has named property getters and setters and NO indexed ones, so '7' is a name; closing this needs a TrySetIndex, or a way for a handler to declare it has no indexed properties at all.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.WrapperIdentityTests.ANodeTakenOutByAnInnerHtmlRewriteIsTheSameObjectWhenItGoesBack' was skipped in the test run.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.WebStorageTests.ADigitOnlyKeyIsANamedPropertyLikeAnyOther' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:01:49.80]       Event types match case-insensitively: the listener maps in src/Broiler.HtmlBridge.Dom/Runtime/EventTargetRegistry.cs:46 and :57 use StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase, so a 'PING' registration answers a 'ping' dispatch where DOM §2.8 keys a listener on a case-sensitive type string.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.EventListenerRegistrationTests.AnEventTypeIsMatchedCaseSensitively' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:01:55.14]       Inline on* handlers fire after every addEventListener listener whenever they were registered: src/Broiler.HtmlBridge.Dom/Features/EventDispatchBinding.cs runs the listener list first and the inline handler afterwards, where HTML §8.1.7.1 registers a content attribute's listener when the attribute is set — at parse time.
Test 'Broiler.HtmlBridge.Tests.EventListenerRegistrationTests.AnInlineHandlerRunsBeforeAListenerAddedAfterIt' was skipped in the test run.
